00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:16.160
So how does this work?

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.614
So when you use
App Engine, it just

00:08:18.614 --> 00:08:20.280
copies over your
application code, built

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:24.460
and on top of the base PHP
image that we've built.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:27.100
And then it deploys the
resulting container.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:27.730
Right?

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:33.780
And this container is built
into Google Container Registry.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.289
GCR dot IO.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:38.950
And if you're not familiar
with that, that's really basic.

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.870
It's just like
Docker Hub, so it's

00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:43.390
like a repository for images.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:46.210
Every time you deploy
a App Engine app,

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.490
it gets put into this URL that
represents your project ID.

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.960
The version and service
that you just built.

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:55.237
So that's really
cool too because you

00:08:55.237 --> 00:08:57.070
can take that image,
and you could deploy it

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:59.770
to Compute Engine or
to Amazon or run it

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:02.120
locally or to Container Engine.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:05.030
Which is what we're going
to talk about later.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:08.860
So some of the advantages
of using our base image

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:12.010
is we automatically install
all of your dependencies,

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:15.790
if you're using Composer, or
you have a Composer dot lock.

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:18.760
Which you all should be doing.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:21.640
We support all the non-end
of life versions of PHP

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:25.870
and will continue to as new
versions become available.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.720
We have good logging and
error-handling integration

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.330
with Google Cloud products.

00:09:31.330 --> 00:09:34.600
We're using Nginx,
which is great.

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:36.110
Apache is also great.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.080
And we don't dislike
Apache and, in fact,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.290
if there was a public
outcry for Apache support

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.000
it would be pretty
trivial to add it.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:45.460
The problem is it's huge.

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:48.820
So it adds like a
significant amount of size

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:51.040
to the resulting
image, which slows down

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:53.220
uploading and deployment
and stuff like that.

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:54.500
Nginx is also great.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:56.200
So we've had a lot
of luck with that.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:57.962
And that's what we
use in the container.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:03.482
So PHP-- any configuration.

00:10:03.482 --> 00:10:05.690
Everyone's going to have
some customization required.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.520
So you can enable a set
of shared extensions

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.680
that we've already
compiled into the runtime.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:16.070
Or you can add whatever custom
configuration that you need.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.920
And you just drop that any file
in the root of your project,

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:20.880
and we load it.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.130
And same thing goes with the
web server configuration.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:28.120
So with Nginx, if you drop
an Nginx dash app dotcom,

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:33.520
we load that configuration and
run that for the container.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:36.130
So some security
changes, since secure

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:39.750
is in the title of this talk.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.250
PHP and your extensions
are kept up to date.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:44.970
So like I said,
all the supported

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:47.100
versions of those extensions--

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:51.000
or sorry of PHP that we have
when minor releases come out,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:52.990
we keep that
regularly up to date.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.698
So when you build on
the player container

00:10:54.698 --> 00:10:55.860
you get the latest version.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.940
We also have security patches.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.360
So a little bit on this,
because I think it's

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.380
a really cool thing
that we've done.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:07.170
How many of you guys have
used parce, underscore, STR?

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.630
Or seen that function?

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:10.675
Nobody?

00:11:10.675 --> 00:11:12.649
Wait this can't be real.

00:11:12.649 --> 00:11:13.440
This guy over here.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:13.940
OK.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:14.790
We've got one.

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.860
This is like a really
common PHP function.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.620
It takes a query string
formatted string and splits it

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.577
apart into key value
pairs in an array.

00:11:23.577 --> 00:11:26.160
So if you haven't used it, all
the libraries that you're using

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.370
have used it.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.620
So this function is great.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.880
But if you don't supply a
second argument, instead

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.740
of loading those
variables into an array,

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.950
it loads them into
the current scope

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:40.800
that the function is being
called in as variables.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.840
So you can see why that would
be a security issue, right?

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.020
If you have a dollar
sign Admin equals false,

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:51.990
and then I type dollars
sign Admin equals true.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.532
In the query string
and that variable

00:11:53.532 --> 00:11:54.656
is going to be overwritten.

00:11:54.656 --> 00:11:56.190
So this is like
not good behavior.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:59.624
This is PHP from
over 10 years ago,

00:11:59.624 --> 00:12:02.040
whenever people were doing
terrible things with registered

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.710
globals and stuff like that.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:05.637
So we don't want that.

00:12:05.637 --> 00:12:06.720
We don't want that at all.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.095
This is what we're trying to
fight as PHP developers, all

00:12:09.095 --> 00:12:10.260
those haters.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.330
They keep saying that
our language isn't secure

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:13.870
because of stuff like this.

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:16.530
So what we've done in our
runtime, is we've patched this.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:17.730
So you can't do this.

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:20.910
You'll just get an error that
you need two argument, which

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.220
is really good.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.590
We also have-- for PHP 5.6 only.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:30.690
Unfortunately, just for PHP 5.6
because it's not stable for 7.0

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:31.560
and above.

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:34.410
We have Suhosin
security extension.

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:39.270
And this is a really, really
good security extension.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.300
Currently what it
does for 5.6 is

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:44.340
it logs potentially unsafe
functions that you might call.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.890
Like [INAUDIBLE] pass
through and things like that.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:48.540
And they'll show up
in cloud loggers.

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:50.430
So you can audit your
own application code

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:52.260
to see how safe it is.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.220
But it does
additional things too.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:13:00.360
It makes sure that your C
extensions-- the C calls

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:03.420
are doing improper
things with memory.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:07.250
Like buffer overflow
and stuff like that.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:10.770
It's good to have that in there.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:13.020
We don't use HT access,
which is generally

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.610
considered not good practice.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:18.120
And we have appropriate
file permissions and user

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:20.040
permissions for the
code that you deploy.

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:23.834
So it's not executing as root,
and the files aren't writable.

00:13:23.834 --> 00:13:24.500
Stuff like that.

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:27.860
OK.

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.370
Talked a lot.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.700
So the two files that
you need-- and this still

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:33.750
isn't App Engine.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:36.910
This is just a container
for your application,

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.070
which is really nice.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.190
The two files that you
need is PHP dot, INI.

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:42.420
This one.

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:46.210
And this is for whatever
PHP configurations you have.

00:13:46.210 --> 00:13:50.381
For this app in particular,
I'm using the GD extension

00:13:50.381 --> 00:13:51.630
to do some image manipulation.

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:53.430
So I've added that
to my INI file.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.770
I've increased the
maximum amount of uploads,

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.620
so that you can upload
100 faces if you want.

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:01.320
And it might explode,
I don't know.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.150
But you can do it.

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.730
We have a few other
extensions that

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:08.940
are installed for WordPress,
BC Math, and Opcache.

00:14:12.096 --> 00:14:13.970
So we also have the
Nginx configuration file.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:16.670
And this is all that's
required for WordPress.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:21.350
And you actually don't need
this if you're using our default

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:22.130
configuration.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:26.090
But I assume that most people
want to add this anyway.

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:29.550
But this basically just points
to the front controller.

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:33.479
So all modern frameworks these
days use front controllers.

00:14:33.479 --> 00:14:35.270
So that's essentially
all this is used for.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:38.750
And then for WordPress you have
a special rewrite for WP Admin.

00:14:41.034 --> 00:14:42.950
So if you're deploying
to App Engine Flex now,

00:14:42.950 --> 00:14:45.420
you need one additional
file, which is app dot yaml.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.910
And if you've used
the App Engine,

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:48.243
you've used app dot yaml before.

00:14:48.243 --> 00:14:50.990
This is basically just
a configuration file

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:56.730
that tells App Engine how
to deploy your application.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.840
So in this case,
we have runtime PHP

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:03.140
to let it know it needs a
build from our PHP base image.

00:15:03.140 --> 00:15:05.840
And we have Mflex to
deploy to App Engine

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:07.760
Flexible environment.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.100
The runtime configuration
thing just says,

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:12.680
our front controllers is
in the root of our project.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:16.190
So if you had Symfony, it
would be document root web.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:19.770
If you had Laravel, it would
be document root public.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.770
And then the rest of this
isn't super important,

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.620
but you can set environment
variables in app.yaml.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:26.380
And if you're using
Cloud SQL version two,

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.510
you declare your
connection there.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:30.530
And it looks like this.

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:32.930
This is basically the
same as what we have,

00:15:32.930 --> 00:15:34.910
but now we have two
separate services.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.370
Instead of two services
contained in an instance.

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.860
So some advantages
of App Engine.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.576
We have versioning with
containers and URLs,

00:15:45.576 --> 00:15:46.450
which is really nice.

00:15:46.450 --> 00:15:49.280
So every time you deploy, you
get a time-stamped version

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:52.340
for the image and a URL as well.

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:54.380
Representing that
version you deployed.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.390
We have autoscaling, of course.

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:59.315
And you have default
SSL and Apps Swapp

00:15:59.315 --> 00:16:02.120
dotcom, which is nice.

