00:18:57.018 --> 00:18:59.268
OK, so we're going to talk
about Container Engine now.

00:19:02.836 --> 00:19:04.710
So moving an application
to Container Engine,

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:06.543
now that you've done
all these other things,

00:19:06.543 --> 00:19:08.499
is hilariously easy.

00:19:08.499 --> 00:19:09.790
There's not even really a step.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:13.500
You could literally take the
image that App Engine uses

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:15.240
and deploy it to
Container Engine.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.710
And you're ready to go.

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:21.240
So it's really simple.

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:23.467
One of the cool features
that we have is--

00:19:23.467 --> 00:19:25.300
let's say you do have
a complicated app.yaml

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:27.000
or something.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.750
You can generate a Docker file
that represents the container

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:34.560
that App Engine builds just
with a single G Cloud command.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.860
That's gcloud, app, gen,
config, dash, dash custom.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:40.589
And so that gets
you a Docker file.

00:19:40.589 --> 00:19:42.630
I haven't really explained
what Dock file is yet,

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:46.650
so that is essentially the
standard Docker container

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.750
format for building
your container.

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:52.080
And I'll show you an example
of that in the next slide.

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:55.440
So after you've done that,
you run a Docker command

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.060
to build the Docker image.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:20:00.090
And then a G Cloud command
to upload that image

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:04.050
to Google Container Registry.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:05.647
Which again, is
like a Docker hub.

00:20:05.647 --> 00:20:07.980
It's just a repository for
all of your container images.

00:20:11.370 --> 00:20:14.360
So this is what you need
for this app in particular.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:19.550
You need a configuration file
for the two different services.

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:23.570
Our WordPress app
and our Worker app.

00:20:23.570 --> 00:20:26.900
And you need a configuration
file that just--

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.680
this just establishes
a load balancer.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.860
So you get an IP
for your service.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.590
This is what a
Dockerfile looks like.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:37.900
This is the one I
use in this app.

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:39.190
It's incredibly basic.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:43.780
All it does is say, we're
using the PHP base image.

00:20:43.780 --> 00:20:47.080
The Google App Engine PHP
base image, so there it is.

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:49.240
And then your
application is going

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:51.940
to be installed in
the app directory.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.224
That's what got generated.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.620
So these are taken by
Kubernetes and Kubernetes

00:20:59.620 --> 00:21:02.050
is container orchestration.

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:05.350
So in comparison
with what we did

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:12.820
in Compute Engine, what we
had was just two containers

00:21:12.820 --> 00:21:15.910
running on the VM.

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:18.940
With Kubernetes, we're
now going to have

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:22.450
a fleet of these containers
that we can scale up and scale

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:23.200
down individually.

00:21:23.200 --> 00:21:26.410
And we have a lot more control
and management with them.

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.330
And I'll demonstrate that later.

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:32.080
But this is the big value add
of using Container Engine.

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:35.230
So this configuration file
is actually really simple.

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:39.580
It may look daunting, but
really all we're doing

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:41.140
is adding some
arbitrary labels here.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:45.105
So we can have fire-grained
control over these containers.

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:56.230
We are just letting it know
that we need a load balancer

00:21:56.230 --> 00:22:01.820
for our WordPress service.

00:22:01.820 --> 00:22:03.920
So there's a WordPress
configuration.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:08.290
You'll see we have a image
path, using GCR dot IO,

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:09.946
so it always pulls
the latest image.

00:22:09.946 --> 00:22:12.320
And we let it know we're
running in the container running

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:13.580
on port 8080.

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:15.260
And we again give
it some labels so it

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.900
knows how to reference
this container later.

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:22.689
And we also have three
of them going on.

00:22:22.689 --> 00:22:23.480
This is our worker.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:25.170
It looks pretty much identical.

00:22:25.170 --> 00:22:29.430
We have a different image
path, different labels for it.

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:33.760
And we're going to
use two of those.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:35.510
So I already showed
you guys how to build.

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:38.090
This is the same thing whenever
you deploy these things.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.400
You run Kube CTL apply.

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:43.520
And Kube CTL actually is
already on your machine

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:44.584
if you use G Cloud.

00:22:44.584 --> 00:22:46.250
And I assume everyone
here uses G Cloud.

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:47.450
So it's already there.

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:49.280
You can just run this
command, and you're

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:53.590
going to deploy these
pods to Container Engine.

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:01.260
The architecture of this
looks pretty much identical

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:02.890
to what we had before.

00:23:02.890 --> 00:23:07.260
Now we just have Container
Engine pods, instead of

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:09.000
App Engine instances.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.730
And the difference here
is pod is basically just

00:23:11.730 --> 00:23:13.640
like this conceptual--

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:18.870
it's one level of abstraction
above the Compute Engine

00:23:18.870 --> 00:23:20.250
instances that
you're running on.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.340
And this just allows you to
have multiple containers running

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.680
in pods, and then you
can take multiple pods

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:29.700
and run them across
your instances.

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:33.100
Your Compute Engine instances.

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:37.500
So demo time.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:38.000
Once again.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:49.350
All right.

