00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:06.750
What I did?

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:24.030
OK, and before I start, I'll
give you a quick background

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:26.790
about Instapage and me.

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.910
I am chief architect
in Instapage.

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:35.100
And I joined Instapage
six years ago.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:41.010
And since it's very beginning,
I've seen growth from the start

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:42.150
to the end.

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:47.190
And Instagpage is the
most powerful landing page

00:31:47.190 --> 00:31:48.960
marketing solution.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:52.060
And it requires
solid infrastructure.

00:31:55.740 --> 00:32:00.060
Our application seems
to be scalable, but not

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:01.450
in the right way.

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:03.720
And speaking to my
colleagues, I had the metaphor

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:07.110
that we have a fleet of buses--

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:11.880
40 seats and one
wheelchair space each one.

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:15.450
And we thought we have
a capacity to scale.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:22.560
But when we've have a spike
in need of wheelchair spaces,

00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:24.030
we are not efficient.

00:32:24.030 --> 00:32:29.400
Because, for example, we have
to send 10 buses for 10 people,

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:33.120
which requires wheelchair space.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:35.560
And referring to our
application, which

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:39.270
the situation was exactly that.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:41.610
We needed something
more flexible.

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:49.530
Because if all traffic came
to our monolithic app and said

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:52.070
there were some bottlenecks.

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:57.720
And tie up application
crashed, costs downtime.

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:01.860
And after experiments with
two Kubernetes and Docker,

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:03.750
it was clear that
the orchestration

00:33:03.750 --> 00:33:05.760
is the right direction.

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:09.150
So our system to
scale, we decided

00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:15.210
to chop it and split it
into small independent micro

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:16.320
services.

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:18.420
But gradually.

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:21.330
First we took out an
[INAUDIBLE] storage system,

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:23.200
which you can see on the right.

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:27.660
And then we made landing
page server system

00:33:27.660 --> 00:33:32.550
for our clients' landing pages.

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:35.550
And as you see on this
diagram, after transition,

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:40.150
our [INAUDIBLE] was smaller.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:43.110
The database with smaller too.

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:47.280
And it had to
handle less traffic.

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:52.680
And most of our traffic was
directed to lightweight.

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:57.118
Lightweight-dedicated and
ultra-scaled microservices.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:11.040
And serving landing pages, we
observed that changes in load

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:13.949
was very viable
and unpredictable.

00:34:13.949 --> 00:34:17.340
And even we had a
lot of spike loads.

00:34:17.340 --> 00:34:24.120
So we needed some kind of
sophisticated automated system

00:34:24.120 --> 00:34:27.060
that could scale
up and scale down.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:31.510
So what we designed was
something like a reverse proxy.

00:34:31.510 --> 00:34:35.799
And we made it and
trapped into Docker to--

00:34:35.799 --> 00:34:36.879
easy to start them.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:43.710
Because start time
was very crucial,

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.770
we even created an
instance template,

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:52.290
which preloaded a Docker
image to spawn them

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:53.969
as fast as possible.

00:34:53.969 --> 00:34:59.400
And then everything we
combine in back end services

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:03.000
with out of scale turn the DOM.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:10.590
And all traffic is going through
the HTP load browser, which I

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:12.810
was asked us to direct traffic.

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:16.860
Direct it zone where
visitor is from.

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:21.960
So visitors from US
will see the content

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:25.710
donated by instances in US.

00:35:25.710 --> 00:35:27.480
From Europe in Europe.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:29.040
And from Asia to Asia.

00:35:31.590 --> 00:35:34.010
And that was a perfect
solution for our needs.

00:35:38.790 --> 00:35:42.840
Each instance has been tested
to and proved that it can

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:46.650
handle 200 requests per second.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:50.280
And with minimal of five because
we have found five zones.

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:51.030
Three in the US.

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:54.180
One in Europe, and one in Asia.

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:57.700
We could handle 1000
requests per second.

00:35:57.700 --> 00:36:00.390
And if traffic exceeds--

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:07.260
it's more than 150 requests
per second for an instance,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:13.600
the system created new
ones to handle the traffic.

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:16.380
And if traffic was
going down, system

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:20.160
was taking down not
needed instances.

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:27.550
And real life traffic, which
looks like sometimes like that.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:31.720
Some customers inform us that
they do expect high load,

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:34.760
but some do not.

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:40.060
And even if with traffic like
that and out of school system

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:46.300
using out of scale
back ends and Docker,

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:52.180
we are able to handle this kind
of traffic without human input.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:03.170
Descale.

00:37:03.170 --> 00:37:03.950
It could be a day.

00:37:03.950 --> 00:37:04.700
It could be a day.

00:37:04.700 --> 00:37:06.837
Normal it looks like an
[INAUDIBLE] sometimes.

