MAREK DAJNOWSKI: Sure.

00:37:09.260 --> 00:37:11.820
BRENT SHAFFER: What's the upper
bounds on that graph there?

00:37:11.820 --> 00:37:12.945
MAREK DAJNOWSKI: Which one?

00:37:12.945 --> 00:37:14.280
BRENT SHAFFER: The upper bounds.

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:15.980
You have 1,000
requests per second,

00:37:15.980 --> 00:37:17.295
and then it goes up to--

00:37:17.295 --> 00:37:18.440
MAREK DAJNOWSKI: --Yes.

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:24.670
I do predict that
some people create

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:26.720
a campaign using AdWords.

00:37:26.720 --> 00:37:30.265
And they tend to stop
it for some reason.

00:37:30.265 --> 00:37:34.640
Or they are also running out.

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:40.160
For some reason, the traffic
drops, and then it comes up.

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.580
If their campaign is resumed
or something like that.

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:53.410
And our system has to handle
500,000 registered users, who

00:37:53.410 --> 00:37:55.780
made 1.5 million pages.

00:37:55.780 --> 00:37:59.260
And traffic to these pages
generates 20 to 50 requests

00:37:59.260 --> 00:37:59.770
per second.

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:07.220
Just normally, when you
see the smallest usage.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:11.860
But the high record we
recorded was 12,000 requests

00:38:11.860 --> 00:38:14.605
per second, and
even over that time

00:38:14.605 --> 00:38:16.780
the system was fully functional.

00:38:16.780 --> 00:38:21.010
Because it created 60 instances.

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:24.580
That was obviously DDOS combined
with [INAUDIBLE] traffic.

00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:31.740
But we had to handle it,
at least for the beginning.

00:38:31.740 --> 00:38:37.830
And we have done great work,
and we have more efficiency

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:39.510
and scalability.

00:38:39.510 --> 00:38:42.090
And much more stable platform.

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:45.480
And we are ready
to grow even more.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:46.480
Thank you.

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:51.840
BRENT SHAFFER: So
