00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:15.860
BRENT SHAFFER: Got you.

00:40:15.860 --> 00:40:17.810
I really like your guys' story.

00:40:17.810 --> 00:40:19.010
I think it's really cool.

00:40:19.010 --> 00:40:25.580
Because it shows where a lot
of PHP applications are, right?

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:30.260
I have personally been involved
with many PHP applications that

00:40:30.260 --> 00:40:33.560
follow this trend of starting
with a massive monolithic

00:40:33.560 --> 00:40:34.250
application.

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:37.250
That was built a
while ago, and it's

00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:39.410
being split off because
it no longer scales well.

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:41.150
Or just because it's
very hard to manage.

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:43.640
So you guys are
in-between, where

00:40:43.640 --> 00:40:46.730
you have moved away from the
monolithic PHP app stage.

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:49.280
But you're still
transitioning to

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:53.960
the fully-orchestrated via
Kubernetes slash Container

00:40:53.960 --> 00:40:56.210
Engine fleet of containers.

00:40:56.210 --> 00:41:03.280
So it's obviously a long process
to move an application to that.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:07.870
You mentioned that you guys
have four or five other

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:09.209
microservices that you're--

00:41:09.209 --> 00:41:10.750
MAREK DAJNOWSKI: In
development, yes.

00:41:10.750 --> 00:41:12.583
BRENT SHAFFER: That
they are in development,

00:41:12.583 --> 00:41:15.020
and that you guys are going
to deploy pretty soon.

00:41:15.020 --> 00:41:18.290
So at that point, you're looking
at a very strong use-case

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:19.540
for something like Kubernetes.

00:41:19.540 --> 00:41:25.665
Because those services are not
going to scale equally, right?

00:41:25.665 --> 00:41:27.790
You're going to want to
scale some more than others

00:41:27.790 --> 00:41:30.172
and everything else.

00:41:30.172 --> 00:41:31.630
One of the advantages
of Kubernetes

00:41:31.630 --> 00:41:33.250
I also wanted to highlight--

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:40.120
using Container
orchestration in general--

00:41:40.120 --> 00:41:43.390
is whenever you're pushing
updates and rolling updates

00:41:43.390 --> 00:41:44.860
for this stuff, like I did.

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:48.490
Which was in a very,
very small scale.

00:41:48.490 --> 00:41:51.100
But when you're talking about
a very big scale, like Marek

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:55.600
is doing, you can use
the tags and the labels--

00:41:55.600 --> 00:41:56.975
you can do rolling updates.

00:41:56.975 --> 00:41:59.600
Kubernetes actually has support
for just doing rolling updates.

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:01.270
So it'll take down
half your pods

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:06.580
and deploy the new pods
pretty much transparently.

00:42:06.580 --> 00:42:09.250
But another advantage is
if you have-- coupling

00:42:09.250 --> 00:42:12.550
between these microservices.

00:42:12.550 --> 00:42:15.290
Where let's say you
have an interface that's

00:42:15.290 --> 00:42:16.840
going to break compatibility.

00:42:16.840 --> 00:42:19.330
And so the way that
these two microservices

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:22.092
talk to each other has to
be updated at the same time.

00:42:22.092 --> 00:42:23.800
Kubernetes makes it
very easy to do that,

00:42:23.800 --> 00:42:25.070
as well, using those labels.

00:42:25.070 --> 00:42:27.730
So as long as you have
the proper labels,

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:30.550
you can roll over those
services together.

00:42:30.550 --> 00:42:33.970
So I'm personally
not a DevOps guy,

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:36.580
which is probably
why I absolutely love

00:42:36.580 --> 00:42:38.080
containerisation in general.

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:42.640
And then also, even more
so, Kubernetes and Container

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:43.360
Engine.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:46.540
Because it makes
me the DevOps guy.

00:42:46.540 --> 00:42:48.094
I don't do the bash scripts.

00:42:48.094 --> 00:42:49.510
At every other job
I've worked at,

00:42:49.510 --> 00:42:52.510
I turn over the application
code to these guys that

00:42:52.510 --> 00:42:55.600
are building RPMs, and they
live inside their terminal.

00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:59.380
And they're super fast
typers and have all hot keys.

00:42:59.380 --> 00:43:01.330
And that's not me.

00:43:01.330 --> 00:43:06.310
But this kind of stuff
makes that very accessible.

00:43:06.310 --> 00:43:11.760
And it makes you
feel like you do have

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:15.160
good oversight to your DevOps.

00:43:15.160 --> 00:43:18.800
So actually, because
we have extra time,

00:43:18.800 --> 00:43:21.360
I'd like to turn it back
over to my demo real quick.

00:43:21.360 --> 00:43:23.880
And show one other thing.

00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:30.900
So you guys already saw
the fleet of containers

00:43:30.900 --> 00:43:34.800
that we deployed
on a command line.

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:38.740
So if you're using
Container Engine--

00:43:44.210 --> 00:43:50.325
we have this great little
dashboard that you can run.

00:43:56.890 --> 00:43:58.430
Oh, I messed up.

00:44:06.800 --> 00:44:09.295
And this helps you
visualize your cluster.

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:16.113
Oh man.

00:44:16.113 --> 00:44:16.988
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:16.988 --> 00:44:18.321
BRENT SHAFFER: Oh, thanks, guys.

00:44:22.470 --> 00:44:25.390
There she is.

00:44:25.390 --> 00:44:29.510
So the reason why this
is indicating a warning

00:44:29.510 --> 00:44:30.760
is because I spun up too many.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:36.250
So I spun up 30, but I only have
four Compute Engine instances

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:37.610
that are available.

00:44:37.610 --> 00:44:41.200
So there's a handful
of these pods that

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:42.850
couldn't be deployed,
because they

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:45.730
have a minimum amount of
requirements that can't be met.

00:44:45.730 --> 00:44:48.420
So whether it's CPU
requirements or something else.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:48.920
Yes.

00:44:48.920 --> 00:44:52.740
This is CPU requirements.

00:44:52.740 --> 00:44:58.340
So one of the primary
tenets of Kubernetes

00:44:58.340 --> 00:45:02.160
is that it abstracts your
container orchestration

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:04.140
from the hardware
that it runs on.

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.610
Or the VMs that it runs on.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:10.450
So the scaling of the pods
happens automatically.

00:45:10.450 --> 00:45:12.430
And it happens
separately from the VMs

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:13.542
that you give Kubernetes.

00:45:13.542 --> 00:45:15.750
Because Kubernetes is going
to take care of all that.

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:16.770
It's going to make
sure that it schedules

00:45:16.770 --> 00:45:19.270
the pods in the right
places and the best places.

00:45:19.270 --> 00:45:24.120
So in this case, if I
gave it a super beefy VM,

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:29.460
and then I gave it a one
CPU, horrible old VM,

00:45:29.460 --> 00:45:32.670
it's going to use that if it
can for resources that it can.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:34.860
To make the best of it.

00:45:34.860 --> 00:45:38.190
So that's pretty much it.

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:40.170
Kubernetes-- it does
get very complicated.

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:41.586
I think one of the
things I wanted

00:45:41.586 --> 00:45:44.640
to show in this presentation
is that it doesn't have to be.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:47.160
There's a lot of really,
really good resources

00:45:47.160 --> 00:45:48.510
out there on Kubernetes.

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:52.140
And so I don't need
to be another one.

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:55.720
There's a udacity course
online on Kubernetes.

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:56.429
That's great.

00:45:56.429 --> 00:45:58.470
There's a lot of YouTube
videos and presentations

00:45:58.470 --> 00:46:00.594
that have happened at other
Google conferences that

00:46:00.594 --> 00:46:01.320
are great.

00:46:01.320 --> 00:46:05.670
But, hopefully, you
guys get the feeling

00:46:05.670 --> 00:46:08.550
that you can take an
existing PHP application,

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:11.100
or whichever one that you're
planning to build next,

00:46:11.100 --> 00:46:13.230
and give this a try.

00:46:13.230 --> 00:46:15.660
Give containers a chance
because they're great.

00:46:15.660 --> 00:46:18.120
And Kubernetes itself
can be a little bit

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:19.800
intimidating at
first, because you do

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.624
have these configuration
files and stuff.

00:46:21.624 --> 00:46:23.290
Which is why App
Engine is great, right?

00:46:23.290 --> 00:46:26.012
You just G Cloud app, deploy.

00:46:26.012 --> 00:46:27.720
Your containers are
running in the cloud.

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:29.460
It's a good way to
dip your toe in.

00:46:29.460 --> 00:46:32.670
And then when you're
ready for the next step,

00:46:32.670 --> 00:46:34.830
you can go ahead and pull
those containers down

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:36.890
and start running a whole
fleet of these things.

00:46:36.890 --> 00:46:37.890
And that's pretty fun.

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:41.840
